(window.RK_DATA = window.RK_DATA || {})["expert-en"] = {"modules":[{"id":"x00","title":"Welcome to the workshop","sub":"How to learn here, home assignments and a warm-up","lessons":[{"id":"x00-1","title":"How Level 3 works","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The workshop","md":"Level 1 taught you to drive. Level 2 opened the hood. Level 3 is the **workshop**: here you make your own parts.\n\nWhat changed:\n- **More practice than theory.** Every lesson has several tasks, and each module ends with a big practice lesson.\n- **Deeper topics.** Your own allocators, your own Future, lock-free code, macros, web, databases.\n- **Home assignments.** Some things don't fit into one Playground file: a web server, your own procedural macro, firmware for a board. You do those on your own computer.\n\nThe rules stay the same: short steps, \"Go deeper\" can be skipped, progress saves by itself.\n\n> **Analogy:** before, you built models from instructions. Now you have a workbench, tools and blueprints — and you machine the parts yourself."},{"t":"text","title":"Home assignments","md":"A \"Home assignment\" card looks like this:\n- what to do — step by step, with `cargo` commands;\n- a **self-check** list: items you tick yourself.\n\nThe site doesn't run that code. The ticks are for you: you can see what's done. You can move on without them.\n\nFor home assignments you need Rust on your computer. It installs with one command from **rustup.rs**."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does the site do with a home assignment?","options":["Checks the code automatically","Remembers the self-check items you ticked","Won't let you move on until everything is ticked"],"answer":1,"why":["No, that code runs on your computer.","Right.","Nothing is locked: you can always move on."]},{"t":"homework","title":"Set up your workbench","md":"Install Rust and check that everything works.\n\n1. Install Rust from **rustup.rs** (Windows: download `rustup-init.exe`; macOS and Linux: one terminal command).\n2. Check the versions:\n```sh\nrustc --version\ncargo --version\n```\n3. Create a project and run it:\n```sh\ncargo new workshop\ncd workshop\ncargo run\n```\n4. Install an editor with hints: VS Code + the **rust-analyzer** extension (or RustRover).","check":["`cargo --version` prints a version","`cargo run` in the new project prints `Hello, world!`","The editor shows hints and underlines errors"]},{"t":"recap","items":["The workshop: lots of practice and deep topics.","Home assignments are done on your computer; you tick the self-check by hand.","Rust installs through rustup.rs."]}],"goals":["See how the workshop differs from the first two levels","Learn what home assignments are"]},{"id":"x00-2","title":"Reading compiler errors like a pro","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"An error is a hint","md":"At Level 3 compiler messages get longer: generics, lifetimes, traits. But they're all built the same way:\n1. The **error code** — `error[E0502]`.\n2. The **main sentence** — what's wrong.\n3. The **arrows** under the code — where exactly.\n4. **help** and **note** — how to fix it. Often there's a ready-made fix.\n\nRead top to bottom and look at the **first** error first: the others are often its consequences.\n\n> **Analogy:** a sat-nav says \"in 200 meters, turn right\". It isn't scolding you — it's showing you the way."},{"t":"text","title":"rustc --explain","md":"Every error has a detailed explanation with examples:\n```sh\nrustc --explain E0502\n```\nThe same is on the web: the \"Rust error codes index\". In the Playground you can just click the error code."},{"t":"code","fails":true,"md":"This code won't compile. Run it and read the error: the code, the main sentence, the arrows, the help.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut list = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    let first = &list[0];\n    list.push(4);\n    println!(\"{first}\");\n}","after":"Error `E0502`: you can't change `list` (`push`) while the reference `first` is alive. Fix it by moving the `println!` above the `push`, or by copying the number: `let first = list[0];`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"The compiler output has five errors. Which do you start with?","options":["The first one","The last one","The shortest one"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. The others often follow from it.","The last ones are usually consequences of the first.","Length says nothing about importance."]},{"t":"task","md":"Fix the code without changing its meaning: first print the first element, then add `4` and print the length.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut list = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    let first = &list[0];\n    list.push(4);\n    println!(\"{first}\");\n    println!(\"{}\", list.len());\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut list = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    let first = list[0];\n    list.push(4);\n    println!(\"{first}\");\n    println!(\"{}\", list.len());\n}","expect":"1\n4","hints":["`i32` is `Copy`. Take a copy of the number instead of a reference: `let first = list[0];`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Fix error `E0382` (use after move): the function should only **look** at the string.","code":"fn shout(s: String) -> String {\n    s.to_uppercase()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"ferris\");\n    println!(\"{}\", shout(name));\n    println!(\"{name}\");\n}","solution":"fn shout(s: &str) -> String {\n    s.to_uppercase()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"ferris\");\n    println!(\"{}\", shout(&name));\n    println!(\"{name}\");\n}","expect":"FERRIS\nferris","hints":["Let the function take `&str`, and call it as `shout(&name)`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Error code, main sentence, arrows, help — read them in order.","Start with the first error.","`rustc --explain E0XXX` — a detailed explanation."]}],"goals":["Break long compiler messages into parts","Use `rustc --explain`"]},{"id":"x00-3","title":"How workshop tasks work","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Tasks got bigger","md":"Many tasks are now checked not by output but by **tests**: the code has a `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`, and the site runs `cargo test`. The task is solved when all tests are green.\n\nThat's how real projects are checked: not \"did it print the right thing\", but \"does the function work on different inputs\"."},{"t":"text","title":"How to solve a big task","md":"1. Read the description and the **tests**: they're more precise than any text.\n2. First get the code to **compile** — `todo!()` inside is fine.\n3. Then fix the tests one at a time.\n4. Stuck? Open a hint. That's not shameful, it's a tool.\n\n> **Analogy:** tests are like a blueprint with dimensions. The part is done when every dimension matches."},{"t":"task","tests":true,"md":"Finish `median` so the tests pass. An empty slice gives `None`; with an even length, it's the average of the two middle values (rounded down).","code":"pub fn median(v: &[i32]) -> Option<i32> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn empty() {\n        assert_eq!(median(&[]), None);\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn odd() {\n        assert_eq!(median(&[5, 1, 3]), Some(3));\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn even() {\n        assert_eq!(median(&[4, 1, 3, 2]), Some(2));\n    }\n}","solution":"pub fn median(v: &[i32]) -> Option<i32> {\n    if v.is_empty() {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let mut s = v.to_vec();\n    s.sort();\n    let n = s.len();\n    if n % 2 == 1 {\n        Some(s[n / 2])\n    } else {\n        Some((s[n / 2 - 1] + s[n / 2]) / 2)\n    }\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn empty() {\n        assert_eq!(median(&[]), None);\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn odd() {\n        assert_eq!(median(&[5, 1, 3]), Some(3));\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn even() {\n        assert_eq!(median(&[4, 1, 3, 2]), Some(2));\n    }\n}","hints":["Copy the slice into a `Vec` (`v.to_vec()`) and sort it.","For `[1, 2, 3, 4]` the middle indexes are `1` and `2`: `(2 + 3) / 2 = 2`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Tasks with tests: solved when all tests pass.","Compile first (with `todo!()` if needed), then fix the tests.","Tests are the most precise task description."]}],"goals":["Learn the format of Level 3 tasks","Get used to tests inside tasks"]},{"id":"x00-p","title":"Practice: workshop warm-up","min":15,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"The `Pairs` iterator yields neighboring pairs of a slice: for `[1, 2, 3]` — `(1, 2)`, `(2, 3)`. Finish `next`.","code":"struct Pairs<'a> {\n    items: &'a [i32],\n    pos: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Iterator for Pairs<'a> {\n    type Item = (i32, i32);\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<(i32, i32)> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<(i32, i32)> = Pairs { items: &[1, 2, 3, 4], pos: 0 }.collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"struct Pairs<'a> {\n    items: &'a [i32],\n    pos: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Iterator for Pairs<'a> {\n    type Item = (i32, i32);\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<(i32, i32)> {\n        if self.pos + 1 >= self.items.len() {\n            return None;\n        }\n        let r = (self.items[self.pos], self.items[self.pos + 1]);\n        self.pos += 1;\n        Some(r)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<(i32, i32)> = Pairs { items: &[1, 2, 3, 4], pos: 0 }.collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"[(1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 4)]","hints":["If `pos + 1` is already past the end — `None`."]},{"t":"task","tests":true,"md":"The `group_by_len` function sorts words by length into a `BTreeMap<usize, Vec<&str>>`. Finish it so the tests pass.","code":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\npub fn group_by_len<'a>(words: &[&'a str]) -> BTreeMap<usize, Vec<&'a str>> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn groups() {\n        let g = group_by_len(&[\"cat\", \"ox\", \"dog\", \"rust\"]);\n        assert_eq!(g[&2], vec![\"ox\"]);\n        assert_eq!(g[&3], vec![\"cat\", \"dog\"]);\n        assert_eq!(g[&4], vec![\"rust\"]);\n    }\n}","solution":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\npub fn group_by_len<'a>(words: &[&'a str]) -> BTreeMap<usize, Vec<&'a str>> {\n    let mut g: BTreeMap<usize, Vec<&'a str>> = BTreeMap::new();\n    for w in words {\n        g.entry(w.chars().count()).or_default().push(*w);\n    }\n    g\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn groups() {\n        let g = group_by_len(&[\"cat\", \"ox\", \"dog\", \"rust\"]);\n        assert_eq!(g[&2], vec![\"ox\"]);\n        assert_eq!(g[&3], vec![\"cat\", \"dog\"]);\n        assert_eq!(g[&4], vec![\"rust\"]);\n    }\n}","hints":["Count the length in characters: `w.chars().count()`.","`g.entry(len).or_default().push(*w)` — creates an empty `Vec` the first time."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write `impl From<&str> for Rgb` that parses `\"#ff8000\"` into three bytes. A bad string gives black, `Rgb(0, 0, 0)`.","code":"#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]\nstruct Rgb(u8, u8, u8);\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Rgb = \"#ff8000\".into();\n    let b: Rgb = \"oops\".into();\n    println!(\"{a:?} {b:?}\");\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]\nstruct Rgb(u8, u8, u8);\n\nimpl From<&str> for Rgb {\n    fn from(s: &str) -> Self {\n        let parse = |i: usize| s.get(i..i + 2).and_then(|h| u8::from_str_radix(h, 16).ok());\n        if s.len() == 7 && s.starts_with('#') {\n            if let (Some(r), Some(g), Some(b)) = (parse(1), parse(3), parse(5)) {\n                return Rgb(r, g, b);\n            }\n        }\n        Rgb(0, 0, 0)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Rgb = \"#ff8000\".into();\n    let b: Rgb = \"oops\".into();\n    println!(\"{a:?} {b:?}\");\n}","expect":"Rgb(255, 128, 0) Rgb(0, 0, 0)","hints":["`u8::from_str_radix(\"ff\", 16)` turns hexadecimal text into a number.","`s.get(1..3)` safely takes a piece of the string and returns `None` if it isn't there."]},{"t":"task","md":"A counter closure: `make_counter()` returns an `impl FnMut() -> u32` that returns the next number on every call, starting at 1.","code":"fn make_counter() -> impl FnMut() -> u32 {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = make_counter();\n    let mut d = make_counter();\n    println!(\"{} {} {}\", c(), c(), c());\n    println!(\"{}\", d());\n}","solution":"fn make_counter() -> impl FnMut() -> u32 {\n    let mut n = 0;\n    move || {\n        n += 1;\n        n\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = make_counter();\n    let mut d = make_counter();\n    println!(\"{} {} {}\", c(), c(), c());\n    println!(\"{}\", d());\n}","expect":"1 2 3\n1","hints":["`let mut n = 0; move || { n += 1; n }` — each counter gets its own `n`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Iterators, `BTreeMap`, `From`, closures with state — the base for the workshop.","If something was hard, revisit that Level 2 module."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Warm up on Level 2 tasks to check yourself"]}]},{"id":"x01","title":"Types, deeper","sub":"Const generics, PhantomData, blanket impls, Sized","lessons":[{"id":"x01-1","title":"Const generics","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A number as a type parameter","md":"Generics can be over a type `<T>`, but also over a **value**: `<const N: usize>`. Then `N` is part of the type, and `Matrix<2>` and `Matrix<3>` are different types.\n\n```\nstruct Buffer<const N: usize> {\n    data: [u8; N],\n}\n```\nYou've seen this already: the array `[T; N]` is exactly a const generic.\n\n> **Analogy:** egg cartons for 6 and for 10. Built the same way, but you can't mix them up: ten eggs don't fit in the six-pack."},{"t":"code","code":"fn sum<const N: usize>(a: [i32; N]) -> i32 {\n    a.iter().sum()\n}\n\nfn first_last<T: Copy, const N: usize>(a: [T; N]) -> Option<(T, T)> {\n    if N == 0 { None } else { Some((a[0], a[N - 1])) }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", sum([1, 2, 3]));\n    println!(\"{}\", sum([10; 5]));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", first_last(['a', 'b', 'c']));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", first_last::<u8, 0>([]));\n}","out":"6\n50\nSome(('a', 'c'))\nNone","after":"`N` is known **at compile time**. `sum` for an array of 3 and of 5 elements are two different copies of the code."},{"t":"text","title":"Where it helps","md":"- Fixed-size vectors and matrices: `Vec3 = Vector<3>`.\n- Heap-free buffers for embedded systems (`heapless::Vec<T, 16>`).\n- Size checks at compile time: the compiler won't let you add a `Vector<3>` and a `Vector<4>`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What is `N` in `struct Ring<const N: usize>`?","options":["A field of the struct","A number that becomes part of the type","A variable you can change"],"answer":1,"why":["It's a type parameter; it isn't stored in memory.","Right. `Ring<4>` and `Ring<8>` are different types.","The value is known at compile time and doesn't change."]},{"t":"task","md":"Make `Vector<const N: usize>` with an `[f64; N]` field and a `dot` method that computes the dot product with another vector of **the same** size.","code":"struct Vector<const N: usize>([f64; N]);\n\nimpl<const N: usize> Vector<N> {\n    fn dot(&self, other: &Vector<N>) -> f64 {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Vector([1.0, 2.0, 3.0]);\n    let b = Vector([4.0, 5.0, 6.0]);\n    println!(\"{}\", a.dot(&b));\n}","solution":"struct Vector<const N: usize>([f64; N]);\n\nimpl<const N: usize> Vector<N> {\n    fn dot(&self, other: &Vector<N>) -> f64 {\n        self.0.iter().zip(other.0.iter()).map(|(x, y)| x * y).sum()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Vector([1.0, 2.0, 3.0]);\n    let b = Vector([4.0, 5.0, 6.0]);\n    println!(\"{}\", a.dot(&b));\n}","expect":"32","hints":["`self.0.iter().zip(other.0.iter()).map(|(x, y)| x * y).sum()`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The ring buffer `Ring<const N: usize>` keeps the last `N` numbers. Finish `push` (overwrites the oldest) and `items` (returns them from oldest to newest).","code":"struct Ring<const N: usize> {\n    buf: [i32; N],\n    start: usize,\n    len: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<const N: usize> Ring<N> {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        Ring { buf: [0; N], start: 0, len: 0 }\n    }\n    fn push(&mut self, x: i32) {\n        // your code\n    }\n    fn items(&self) -> Vec<i32> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut r: Ring<3> = Ring::new();\n    for x in 1..=5 {\n        r.push(x);\n    }\n    println!(\"{:?}\", r.items());\n}","solution":"struct Ring<const N: usize> {\n    buf: [i32; N],\n    start: usize,\n    len: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<const N: usize> Ring<N> {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        Ring { buf: [0; N], start: 0, len: 0 }\n    }\n    fn push(&mut self, x: i32) {\n        if self.len < N {\n            self.buf[(self.start + self.len) % N] = x;\n            self.len += 1;\n        } else {\n            self.buf[self.start] = x;\n            self.start = (self.start + 1) % N;\n        }\n    }\n    fn items(&self) -> Vec<i32> {\n        (0..self.len).map(|i| self.buf[(self.start + i) % N]).collect()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut r: Ring<3> = Ring::new();\n    for x in 1..=5 {\n        r.push(x);\n    }\n    println!(\"{:?}\", r.items());\n}","expect":"[3, 4, 5]","hints":["While the buffer isn't full, write at `(start + len) % N` and increase `len`.","When it's full, write at `start` (the oldest) and move `start` by one."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`<const N: usize>` — a generic over a number. `N` is part of the type.","Functions for arrays of any length: `fn f<const N: usize>(a: [T; N])`.","Sizes are checked at compile time."]}],"goals":["Make generics over numbers, not just types","Write functions for arrays of any length"]},{"id":"x01-2","title":"PhantomData and marker types","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Same data, different meaning","md":"`Distance<Meters>` and `Distance<Feet>` both hold the same `f64` inside. But you can't add them together.\n\nThe `Unit` parameter isn't used in the data. The compiler doesn't like that: \"parameter is never used\". That's what `PhantomData<Unit>` is for — a zero-size field that says \"this type is sort of here\".\n\n> **Analogy:** colored stickers on identical jars. Same jar, but red is for spices and blue is for grains."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::marker::PhantomData;\nuse std::ops::Add;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Meters;\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Feet;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Distance<U> {\n    value: f64,\n    unit: PhantomData<U>,\n}\n\nimpl<U> Distance<U> {\n    fn new(value: f64) -> Self {\n        Distance { value, unit: PhantomData }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<U> Add for Distance<U> {\n    type Output = Distance<U>;\n    fn add(self, o: Self) -> Self {\n        Distance::new(self.value + o.value)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Distance<Meters> = Distance::new(5.0);\n    let b: Distance<Meters> = Distance::new(2.5);\n    println!(\"{}\", (a + b).value);\n    println!(\"{}\", std::mem::size_of::<Distance<Feet>>());\n    // let c: Distance<Feet> = Distance::new(1.0);\n    // a + c; // error: different units\n}","out":"7.5\n8","after":"`Distance<Feet>` is 8 bytes, like a single `f64`. The marker weighs nothing."},{"t":"quiz","md":"How much memory does a `PhantomData<T>` field take?","options":["Zero bytes","As much as `T`","One pointer"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. It's only information for the compiler.","No, `T` isn't stored there.","There's no pointer at all."]},{"t":"task","md":"Add a `to_feet` method to `Distance<Meters>` that returns a `Distance<Feet>` (1 m = 3.28084 ft). The method must exist **only** on meters.","code":"use std::marker::PhantomData;\n\nstruct Meters;\nstruct Feet;\n\nstruct Distance<U> {\n    value: f64,\n    unit: PhantomData<U>,\n}\n\nimpl<U> Distance<U> {\n    fn new(value: f64) -> Self {\n        Distance { value, unit: PhantomData }\n    }\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let m: Distance<Meters> = Distance::new(10.0);\n    let f: Distance<Feet> = m.to_feet();\n    println!(\"{:.1}\", f.value);\n}","solution":"use std::marker::PhantomData;\n\nstruct Meters;\nstruct Feet;\n\nstruct Distance<U> {\n    value: f64,\n    unit: PhantomData<U>,\n}\n\nimpl<U> Distance<U> {\n    fn new(value: f64) -> Self {\n        Distance { value, unit: PhantomData }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Distance<Meters> {\n    fn to_feet(&self) -> Distance<Feet> {\n        Distance::new(self.value * 3.28084)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let m: Distance<Meters> = Distance::new(10.0);\n    let f: Distance<Feet> = m.to_feet();\n    println!(\"{:.1}\", f.value);\n}","expect":"32.8","hints":["A separate `impl Distance<Meters> { ... }` block — methods only for meters."]},{"t":"task","md":"Typed identifiers: `Id<User>` and `Id<Order>` both hold a `u64`, but can't be mixed up. Finish `Id::new` and the `find_user` function, which accepts only `Id<User>`.","code":"use std::marker::PhantomData;\n\nstruct User;\nstruct Order;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nstruct Id<T> {\n    raw: u64,\n    kind: PhantomData<T>,\n}\n\nimpl<T> Id<T> {\n    // your code: fn new(raw: u64) -> Self\n}\n\nfn find_user(id: Id<User>) -> &'static str {\n    // return \"Ada\" if raw == 1, otherwise \"nobody\"\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let u: Id<User> = Id::new(1);\n    let _o: Id<Order> = Id::new(1);\n    println!(\"{}\", find_user(u));\n    println!(\"{}\", find_user(Id::new(7)));\n}","solution":"use std::marker::PhantomData;\n\nstruct User;\nstruct Order;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nstruct Id<T> {\n    raw: u64,\n    kind: PhantomData<T>,\n}\n\nimpl<T> Id<T> {\n    fn new(raw: u64) -> Self {\n        Id { raw, kind: PhantomData }\n    }\n}\n\nfn find_user(id: Id<User>) -> &'static str {\n    if id.raw == 1 { \"Ada\" } else { \"nobody\" }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let u: Id<User> = Id::new(1);\n    let _o: Id<Order> = Id::new(1);\n    println!(\"{}\", find_user(u));\n    println!(\"{}\", find_user(Id::new(7)));\n}","expect":"Ada\nnobody","hints":["`fn new(raw: u64) -> Self { Id { raw, kind: PhantomData } }`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"derive and PhantomData: a catch","md":"`#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]` on `Id<T>` requires that **`T` too** is `Clone`/`Copy`/`PartialEq`. Even though `T` isn't stored inside! That's how `derive` works: it adds a bound on every parameter.\n\nSo the `Id<User>` from the task won't be `Copy` unless `User` is `Copy`. The workaround is a manual implementation:\n\n```\nimpl<T> Clone for Id<T> { fn clone(&self) -> Self { *self } }\nimpl<T> Copy for Id<T> {}\n```\nOr crates like `derive-where`, where you can state the bounds by hand."},{"t":"recap","items":["`PhantomData<T>` is a zero-size type marker.","Same data + different markers = different types.","`impl Type<Marker>` — methods only for a specific marker."]}],"goals":["Tag a type with information that isn't in the data","Make units of measure that can't be mixed up"]},{"id":"x01-3","title":"Blanket impls and extension traits","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"An implementation \"for everyone\"","md":"A **blanket impl** implements a trait for **any** type that meets a condition:\n\n```\nimpl<T: Display> MyTrait for T { ... }\n```\nThe standard library is built this way: `impl<T: Display> ToString for T` — that's why everything that can `Display` has `.to_string()`."},{"t":"text","title":"Extension trait","md":"You can't add a method directly to `str` or `Vec` — they aren't yours. But you can declare **your own** trait with a method and implement it for someone else's type. This is called an **extension trait**:\n\n```\ntrait Shout {\n    fn shout(&self) -> String;\n}\nimpl Shout for str {\n    fn shout(&self) -> String { self.to_uppercase() + \"!\" }\n}\n// \"hello\".shout() == \"HELLO!\"\n```\nThe orphan rule isn't broken: the trait is yours.\n\n> **Analogy:** a drill attachment. The drill isn't yours, but the attachment is — and the drill gets a new function."},{"t":"code","md":"An extension for **every iterator** of numbers — a blanket impl and an extension trait together:","code":"trait Stats: Iterator<Item = i32> + Sized {\n    fn mean(self) -> Option<f64> {\n        let (sum, n) = self.fold((0i64, 0u32), |(s, n), x| (s + x as i64, n + 1));\n        if n == 0 { None } else { Some(sum as f64 / n as f64) }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<I: Iterator<Item = i32>> Stats for I {}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", vec![1, 2, 3, 4].into_iter().mean());\n    println!(\"{:?}\", (1..=10).filter(|x| x % 2 == 0).mean());\n    println!(\"{:?}\", std::iter::empty::<i32>().mean());\n}","out":"Some(2.5)\nSome(6.0)\nNone","after":"Now **any** `i32` iterator has `.mean()` — even ones that don't exist yet."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why does every type with `Display` have a `.to_string()` method?","options":["Someone wrote it by hand for each type","The standard library has a blanket impl of `ToString` for all `T: Display`","The compiler adds it by itself"],"answer":1,"why":["That would be impossible.","Right.","It's ordinary library code."]},{"t":"task","md":"Make an extension trait `CountWords` with a method `word_count(&self) -> usize` and implement it for `str`.","code":"// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", \"  the cat   sat \".word_count());\n    let s = String::from(\"one two\");\n    println!(\"{}\", s.word_count());\n}","solution":"trait CountWords {\n    fn word_count(&self) -> usize;\n}\n\nimpl CountWords for str {\n    fn word_count(&self) -> usize {\n        self.split_whitespace().count()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", \"  the cat   sat \".word_count());\n    let s = String::from(\"one two\");\n    println!(\"{}\", s.word_count());\n}","expect":"3\n2","hints":["`impl CountWords for str { ... }` — and `String` gets the method too, through `Deref` to `str`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A blanket impl: the `Describe` trait has a method `describe(&self) -> String` that returns `<value> (N chars)`. Implement it for all `T: Display` at once.","code":"use std::fmt::Display;\n\ntrait Describe {\n    fn describe(&self) -> String;\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", 42.describe());\n    println!(\"{}\", \"crab\".describe());\n}","solution":"use std::fmt::Display;\n\ntrait Describe {\n    fn describe(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nimpl<T: Display> Describe for T {\n    fn describe(&self) -> String {\n        let s = self.to_string();\n        format!(\"{} ({} chars)\", s, s.chars().count())\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", 42.describe());\n    println!(\"{}\", \"crab\".describe());\n}","expect":"42 (2 chars)\ncrab (4 chars)","hints":["`impl<T: Display> Describe for T { ... }`.","Inside: `let s = self.to_string();` and `s.chars().count()`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Blanket impl: `impl<T: Condition> Trait for T`.","An extension trait adds methods to other people's types.","Together they give methods to whole families of types."]}],"goals":["Implement a trait for all fitting types at once","Add your own methods to other people's types"]},{"id":"x01-4","title":"Sized and ?Sized","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Not every type has a known size","md":"Most types have a size known at compile time: `i32` is 4 bytes, `String` is 24 bytes. That's the `Sized` trait.\n\nBut some types have **no** known size:\n- `str` — a string of any length;\n- `[T]` — a slice of any length;\n- `dyn Trait` — some type with a trait.\n\nYou can't put them in a variable directly — only behind a pointer: `&str`, `Box<[T]>`, `&dyn Trait`."},{"t":"text","title":"Implicit Sized","md":"Every `<T>` in a generic really means `<T: Sized>`. The compiler adds it by itself. To allow unsized types as well, you write `?Sized` — \"maybe not Sized\":\n\n```\nfn show<T: Display + ?Sized>(x: &T) { println!(\"{x}\"); }\n```\nNow `show` accepts `&i32`, `&str` and `&dyn Display`.\n\n> **Analogy:** a fixed-size suitcase (`Sized`) versus luggage \"of any size, but only on a trolley\" (`?Sized` behind a pointer)."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::fmt::Display;\n\nfn show<T: Display + ?Sized>(label: &str, x: &T) {\n    println!(\"{label}: {x} ({} bytes behind the reference)\", std::mem::size_of_val(x));\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    show(\"number\", &7u8);\n    show(\"string\", \"hello\");\n    let d: &dyn Display = &3.5f64;\n    show(\"dyn\", d);\n}","out":"number: 7 (1 bytes behind the reference)\nstring: hello (5 bytes behind the reference)\ndyn: 3.5 (8 bytes behind the reference)","after":"`size_of_val` finds the size at run time: the string \"hello\" is 5 bytes."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `T: ?Sized` mean?","options":["`T` definitely has no size","`T` may be unsized, so it's used only behind a pointer","`T` is always on the heap"],"answer":1,"why":["Not \"definitely without\", but \"may be without\".","Right.","Not necessarily: a `&str` can point anywhere."]},{"t":"task","md":"`longest_of` must accept a slice of **anything** that can turn into `&str` (`String`, `&str`) and return the longest length in characters. `print_all` must also accept a `str`. Add the missing bound.","code":"fn longest_of<T: AsRef<str>>(items: &[T]) -> usize {\n    items.iter().map(|s| s.as_ref().chars().count()).max().unwrap_or(0)\n}\n\nfn print_all<T: std::fmt::Display>(items: &T) {\n    println!(\"{items}\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", longest_of(&[\"cat\", \"crocodile\"]));\n    println!(\"{}\", longest_of(&[String::from(\"ab\")]));\n    print_all(\"an unsized string\");\n}","solution":"fn longest_of<T: AsRef<str>>(items: &[T]) -> usize {\n    items.iter().map(|s| s.as_ref().chars().count()).max().unwrap_or(0)\n}\n\nfn print_all<T: std::fmt::Display + ?Sized>(items: &T) {\n    println!(\"{items}\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", longest_of(&[\"cat\", \"crocodile\"]));\n    println!(\"{}\", longest_of(&[String::from(\"ab\")]));\n    print_all(\"an unsized string\");\n}","expect":"9\n2\nan unsized string","hints":["`print_all(\"...\")` passes a `&str`, so `T = str` — unsized. You need `?Sized`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`Sized` — the size is known at compile time. Every `<T>` has it by default.","`str`, `[T]`, `dyn Trait` are unsized and live behind a pointer.","`T: ?Sized` + `&T` — accept such types too."]}],"goals":["Understand what a type without a known size is","Write functions that accept `str`, `[T]` and `dyn Trait`"]},{"id":"x01-p","title":"Practice: types","min":20,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"A matrix `Matrix<const R: usize, const C: usize>` and its transpose: an `R×C` matrix becomes `C×R`. Finish `transpose`.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Matrix<const R: usize, const C: usize>([[i32; C]; R]);\n\nimpl<const R: usize, const C: usize> Matrix<R, C> {\n    fn transpose(&self) -> Matrix<C, R> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let m = Matrix([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", m.transpose());\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Matrix<const R: usize, const C: usize>([[i32; C]; R]);\n\nimpl<const R: usize, const C: usize> Matrix<R, C> {\n    fn transpose(&self) -> Matrix<C, R> {\n        let mut out = [[0; R]; C];\n        for r in 0..R {\n            for c in 0..C {\n                out[c][r] = self.0[r][c];\n            }\n        }\n        Matrix(out)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let m = Matrix([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", m.transpose());\n}","expect":"Matrix([[1, 4], [2, 5], [3, 6]])","hints":["Create `[[0; R]; C]` and copy `out[c][r] = self.0[r][c]`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Money in different currencies: `Money<Usd>` and `Money<Eur>` can't be added together. Implement `Add` only for the same currency, and `Display`, which prints the amount with the currency code from the associated constant `CODE`.","code":"use std::fmt;\nuse std::marker::PhantomData;\nuse std::ops::Add;\n\ntrait Currency {\n    const CODE: &'static str;\n}\nstruct Usd;\nstruct Eur;\nimpl Currency for Usd { const CODE: &'static str = \"USD\"; }\nimpl Currency for Eur { const CODE: &'static str = \"EUR\"; }\n\nstruct Money<C> {\n    cents: i64,\n    cur: PhantomData<C>,\n}\n\nimpl<C> Money<C> {\n    fn new(cents: i64) -> Self {\n        Money { cents, cur: PhantomData }\n    }\n}\n\n// your code: Add and Display\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Money<Usd> = Money::new(1050);\n    let b: Money<Usd> = Money::new(275);\n    println!(\"{}\", a + b);\n    println!(\"{}\", Money::<Eur>::new(99));\n}","solution":"use std::fmt;\nuse std::marker::PhantomData;\nuse std::ops::Add;\n\ntrait Currency {\n    const CODE: &'static str;\n}\nstruct Usd;\nstruct Eur;\nimpl Currency for Usd { const CODE: &'static str = \"USD\"; }\nimpl Currency for Eur { const CODE: &'static str = \"EUR\"; }\n\nstruct Money<C> {\n    cents: i64,\n    cur: PhantomData<C>,\n}\n\nimpl<C> Money<C> {\n    fn new(cents: i64) -> Self {\n        Money { cents, cur: PhantomData }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<C> Add for Money<C> {\n    type Output = Money<C>;\n    fn add(self, o: Self) -> Self {\n        Money::new(self.cents + o.cents)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<C: Currency> fmt::Display for Money<C> {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        write!(f, \"{}.{:02} {}\", self.cents / 100, self.cents % 100, C::CODE)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Money<Usd> = Money::new(1050);\n    let b: Money<Usd> = Money::new(275);\n    println!(\"{}\", a + b);\n    println!(\"{}\", Money::<Eur>::new(99));\n}","expect":"13.25 USD\n0.99 EUR","hints":["`impl<C> Add for Money<C>` — only matching `C`s add up.","`Display` needs `C: Currency` to read `C::CODE`."]},{"t":"task","md":"An extension trait `ChunkSum` for **all slices** of numbers: the method `chunk_sums(n)` returns the sums of chunks of `n` items. Write a blanket implementation for `[T]` where `T: Copy + std::iter::Sum`.","code":"trait ChunkSum<T> {\n    fn chunk_sums(&self, n: usize) -> Vec<T>;\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].chunk_sums(2));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", vec![0.5, 1.5, 2.0].chunk_sums(3));\n}","solution":"trait ChunkSum<T> {\n    fn chunk_sums(&self, n: usize) -> Vec<T>;\n}\n\nimpl<T: Copy + std::iter::Sum> ChunkSum<T> for [T] {\n    fn chunk_sums(&self, n: usize) -> Vec<T> {\n        self.chunks(n).map(|c| c.iter().copied().sum()).collect()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].chunk_sums(2));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", vec![0.5, 1.5, 2.0].chunk_sums(3));\n}","expect":"[3, 7, 5]\n[4.0]","hints":["`impl<T: Copy + std::iter::Sum> ChunkSum<T> for [T]`.","`self.chunks(n).map(|c| c.iter().copied().sum()).collect()`."]},{"t":"task","md":"`print_ref` prints any value, including unsized ones and `dyn Display`. Fix its signature so every call compiles.","code":"use std::fmt::Display;\n\nfn print_ref<T: Display>(x: &T) {\n    println!(\"> {x}\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    print_ref(&5);\n    print_ref(\"str\");\n    let items: Vec<Box<dyn Display>> = vec![Box::new(1.5), Box::new(\"two\")];\n    for it in &items {\n        print_ref(it.as_ref());\n    }\n}","solution":"use std::fmt::Display;\n\nfn print_ref<T: Display + ?Sized>(x: &T) {\n    println!(\"> {x}\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    print_ref(&5);\n    print_ref(\"str\");\n    let items: Vec<Box<dyn Display>> = vec![Box::new(1.5), Box::new(\"two\")];\n    for it in &items {\n        print_ref(it.as_ref());\n    }\n}","expect":"> 5\n> str\n> 1.5\n> two","hints":["Both `str` and `dyn Display` are unsized. Add `?Sized`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Constants in types — sizes checked at compile time.","`PhantomData` + associated constants — markers that carry data for printing.","A blanket impl for `[T]` — methods for every slice.","`?Sized` — accept `str` and `dyn Trait`."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Use const generics, PhantomData, blanket impls and ?Sized"]}]},{"id":"x02","title":"Fine points of traits","sub":"dyn compatibility, upcasting, Any, GATs, impl Trait and async fn in traits","lessons":[{"id":"x02-1","title":"What dyn can and can't do","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The dyn compatibility rules","md":"To use a trait as `dyn Trait`, the compiler must build a **method table**. That's impossible if a method:\n- returns `Self` (the size of `Self` is unknown behind `dyn`);\n- is generic over its own parameter `<T>` (that would need an endless table — one entry per `T`);\n- doesn't take `self` at all (a static method like `fn new()`).\n\nSuch a trait is called **not dyn compatible**.\n\n> **Analogy:** a TV remote: its buttons are known in advance. You can't put a \"do that thing, whatever it is\" button on a remote."},{"t":"text","title":"Switch a method off for dyn","md":"If a trait has one \"awkward\" method, you can **exclude** it from the table:\n\n```\ntrait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64;\n    fn scaled(&self, k: f64) -> Self where Self: Sized; // not for dyn\n}\n```\n`where Self: Sized` means \"this method is only for concrete types\". The other methods still work through `dyn Shape`."},{"t":"code","code":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64;\n    fn name(&self) -> String {\n        String::from(\"shape\")\n    }\n    fn scaled(&self, k: f64) -> Self\n    where\n        Self: Sized;\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Square(f64);\n\nimpl Shape for Square {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64 { self.0 * self.0 }\n    fn scaled(&self, k: f64) -> Self { Square(self.0 * k) }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let big = Square(2.0).scaled(3.0);\n    println!(\"{big:?}\");\n    let shapes: Vec<Box<dyn Shape>> = vec![Box::new(big), Box::new(Square(1.0))];\n    for s in &shapes {\n        println!(\"{}: {}\", s.name(), s.area());\n    }\n}","out":"Square(6.0)\nshape: 36\nshape: 1","after":"Remove `where Self: Sized` and look at the error: the trait stops being dyn compatible."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which method makes a trait not dyn compatible?","options":["`fn area(&self) -> f64`","`fn clone_me(&self) -> Self`","`fn name(&self) -> String`"],"answer":1,"why":["An ordinary method, it's fine.","Right. The size of `Self` is unknown behind `dyn`.","`String` is a known type, it's fine."]},{"t":"task","md":"The `Animal` trait doesn't compile as `dyn` because it has `fn new(name: &str) -> Self`. Exclude that method from the table so `Vec<Box<dyn Animal>>` works.","code":"trait Animal {\n    fn new(name: &str) -> Self;\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Cat(String);\n\nimpl Animal for Cat {\n    fn new(name: &str) -> Self { Cat(name.to_string()) }\n    fn speak(&self) -> String { format!(\"{}: meow\", self.0) }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let zoo: Vec<Box<dyn Animal>> = vec![Box::new(Cat::new(\"Kitty\"))];\n    for a in &zoo {\n        println!(\"{}\", a.speak());\n    }\n}","solution":"trait Animal {\n    fn new(name: &str) -> Self\n    where\n        Self: Sized;\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Cat(String);\n\nimpl Animal for Cat {\n    fn new(name: &str) -> Self { Cat(name.to_string()) }\n    fn speak(&self) -> String { format!(\"{}: meow\", self.0) }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let zoo: Vec<Box<dyn Animal>> = vec![Box::new(Cat::new(\"Kitty\"))];\n    for a in &zoo {\n        println!(\"{}\", a.speak());\n    }\n}","expect":"Kitty: meow","hints":["Add the condition `where Self: Sized` to `new`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`dyn` needs a trait without `-> Self`, without generic methods and without methods lacking `self`.","`where Self: Sized` removes a method from the `dyn` table.","The other methods still work through `dyn`."]}],"goals":["See why not every trait can be used as `dyn`","Exclude single methods with `where Self: Sized`"]},{"id":"x02-2","title":"Upcasting and Any","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Upcasting","md":"If `trait Pet: Animal` (Pet requires Animal), any `&dyn Pet` can be turned into a `&dyn Animal`. That's **upcasting** — moving to a more general trait. You write it as a plain assignment.\n\n```\nlet p: &dyn Pet = &dog;\nlet a: &dyn Animal = p; // upcast\n```"},{"t":"code","code":"trait Animal {\n    fn name(&self) -> String;\n}\ntrait Pet: Animal {\n    fn owner(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Dog;\nimpl Animal for Dog {\n    fn name(&self) -> String { String::from(\"Rex\") }\n}\nimpl Pet for Dog {\n    fn owner(&self) -> String { String::from(\"Uncle Theo\") }\n}\n\nfn describe(a: &dyn Animal) {\n    println!(\"animal: {}\", a.name());\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let pets: Vec<Box<dyn Pet>> = vec![Box::new(Dog)];\n    for p in &pets {\n        println!(\"owner: {}\", p.owner());\n        describe(p.as_ref()); // &dyn Pet -> &dyn Animal\n    }\n}","out":"owner: Uncle Theo\nanimal: Rex"},{"t":"text","title":"Any — find out who's in there","md":"`std::any::Any` is a trait almost every type has. Through a `dyn Any` you can ask \"are you a `String`?\" — and get a reference to the concrete type.\n\n```\nuse std::any::Any;\nfn inspect(x: &dyn Any) {\n    if let Some(s) = x.downcast_ref::<String>() { println!(\"string {s}\"); }\n}\n```\nThis is **downcasting** — moving to a concrete type. It's used rarely: an `enum` is usually better. But for plugins and mixed storage it's a lifesaver."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::any::Any;\n\nfn inspect(x: &dyn Any) -> String {\n    if let Some(n) = x.downcast_ref::<i32>() {\n        format!(\"number {n}\")\n    } else if let Some(s) = x.downcast_ref::<String>() {\n        format!(\"string \\\"{s}\\\"\")\n    } else {\n        String::from(\"something else\")\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let items: Vec<Box<dyn Any>> = vec![Box::new(7), Box::new(String::from(\"cat\")), Box::new(1.5)];\n    for it in &items {\n        println!(\"{}\", inspect(it.as_ref()));\n    }\n}","out":"number 7\nstring \"cat\"\nsomething else"},{"t":"quiz","md":"What is downcasting?","options":["Turning a `&dyn Pet` into a `&dyn Animal`","Trying to get a concrete type out of a `dyn Any`","Casting a number to a smaller type"],"answer":1,"why":["That's upcasting: to something more general.","Right. It may fail — hence the `Option`.","That's `as`, a different thing."]},{"t":"task","md":"A component store: `Vec<Box<dyn Any>>`. Write `sum_ints`, which adds up only the values of type `i32` and skips the rest.","code":"use std::any::Any;\n\nfn sum_ints(items: &[Box<dyn Any>]) -> i32 {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let items: Vec<Box<dyn Any>> = vec![\n        Box::new(10),\n        Box::new(\"text\"),\n        Box::new(5),\n        Box::new(2.5f64),\n    ];\n    println!(\"{}\", sum_ints(&items));\n}","solution":"use std::any::Any;\n\nfn sum_ints(items: &[Box<dyn Any>]) -> i32 {\n    items.iter().filter_map(|b| b.downcast_ref::<i32>()).sum()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let items: Vec<Box<dyn Any>> = vec![\n        Box::new(10),\n        Box::new(\"text\"),\n        Box::new(5),\n        Box::new(2.5f64),\n    ];\n    println!(\"{}\", sum_ints(&items));\n}","expect":"15","hints":["`b.downcast_ref::<i32>()` returns an `Option<&i32>`. `filter_map` fits."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Upcasting: `&dyn Sub` → `&dyn Super` when `trait Sub: Super`.","`dyn Any` + `downcast_ref::<T>()` — find out the concrete type.","Downcasting is a last resort; an `enum` is usually better."]}],"goals":["Turn a `&dyn Sub` into a `&dyn Super`","Find out the concrete type behind a `dyn Any`"]},{"id":"x02-3","title":"GATs: a lending iterator","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The problem: an iterator can't lend itself","md":"A normal `Iterator` yields items that live **independently** of the `next` call. So you can't write an iterator that returns a `&mut` into **its own** buffer on every step: two such items at once would be two `&mut` to the same thing.\n\nYou need a trait where the item type **depends on how long the iterator itself is borrowed**:\n\n```\ntrait LendingIterator {\n    type Item<'a> where Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item<'_>>;\n}\n```\n`type Item<'a>` is a **generic associated type** (GAT).\n\n> **Analogy:** a librarian lets you read a book in the reading room. You get the next one only after you return this one."},{"t":"code","code":"trait LendingIterator {\n    type Item<'a>\n    where\n        Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item<'_>>;\n}\n\n// Windows of 2 items that you can CHANGE\nstruct WindowsMut<'s> {\n    data: &'s mut [i32],\n    pos: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<'s> LendingIterator for WindowsMut<'s> {\n    type Item<'a> = &'a mut [i32] where Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&mut [i32]> {\n        let start = self.pos;\n        if start + 2 > self.data.len() {\n            return None;\n        }\n        self.pos += 1;\n        Some(&mut self.data[start..start + 2])\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut v = [1, 2, 3, 4];\n    let mut it = WindowsMut { data: &mut v, pos: 0 };\n    while let Some(w) = it.next() {\n        w[1] += w[0]; // running total\n    }\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","out":"[1, 3, 6, 10]","after":"The regular `windows` gives only `&[T]`. Changing through windows became possible thanks to GATs."},{"t":"text","title":"The while let limitation","md":"`for`, `map` and `collect` don't work with a `LendingIterator` — they're built for the normal `Iterator`. You write `while let Some(x) = it.next()`. In exchange it's safe: you can't get the next item while the previous one is alive."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why can't a normal `Iterator` yield `&mut` into its own buffer?","options":["`collect` could gather several `&mut` to the same data at once","You can't return `&mut` from methods at all","The buffer must be on the heap"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. A normal iterator's items live independently of each other.","You can, just not this way.","The heap has nothing to do with it."]},{"t":"task","md":"Finish the `LendingIterator` for `Upper`: each call puts the next line of text, uppercased, into the **internal** `String` buffer and returns a `&str` to that buffer.","code":"trait LendingIterator {\n    type Item<'a>\n    where\n        Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item<'_>>;\n}\n\nstruct Upper<'t> {\n    lines: std::str::Lines<'t>,\n    buf: String,\n}\n\nimpl<'t> LendingIterator for Upper<'t> {\n    type Item<'a> = &'a str where Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&str> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"one\\ntwo\\nthree\";\n    let mut it = Upper { lines: text.lines(), buf: String::new() };\n    while let Some(line) = it.next() {\n        println!(\"{line}\");\n    }\n}","solution":"trait LendingIterator {\n    type Item<'a>\n    where\n        Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item<'_>>;\n}\n\nstruct Upper<'t> {\n    lines: std::str::Lines<'t>,\n    buf: String,\n}\n\nimpl<'t> LendingIterator for Upper<'t> {\n    type Item<'a> = &'a str where Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&str> {\n        let line = self.lines.next()?;\n        self.buf.clear();\n        for c in line.chars() {\n            self.buf.extend(c.to_uppercase());\n        }\n        Some(&self.buf)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"one\\ntwo\\nthree\";\n    let mut it = Upper { lines: text.lines(), buf: String::new() };\n    while let Some(line) = it.next() {\n        println!(\"{line}\");\n    }\n}","expect":"ONE\nTWO\nTHREE","hints":["Take a line: `let line = self.lines.next()?;`. Then `self.buf.clear()` and fill the buffer.","Return a reference to the buffer: `Some(&self.buf)`. One buffer for every step — no copies."]},{"t":"deep","title":"GATs go beyond iterators","md":"GATs are needed wherever an associated type must depend on a parameter: a lifetime or a type.\n\n- **Collection \"families\"**: `type Pointer<T>` — a trait that says \"my smart pointer is `Rc<T>`\" or \"`Arc<T>`\", and code is written for both.\n- **Async traits**, before `async fn` in traits existed, used `type Fut<'a>: Future + 'a`.\n- **Zero-copy parsers** that return pieces of their own buffer.\n\nThe required `where Self: 'a` says: \"a reference to the item won't outlive the iterator itself\". The compiler wants it written explicitly."},{"t":"recap","items":["GAT: `type Item<'a> where Self: 'a;` — the item type depends on the borrow.","A `LendingIterator` yields references into its buffer, one at a time.","Use it with `while let`, not `for`."]}],"goals":["Understand an associated type with a parameter","Write an iterator that yields references into its own buffer"]},{"id":"x02-4","title":"impl Trait and async fn in traits","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Returning impl Trait from a trait method","md":"A trait can have a method that returns \"some iterator\" — each implementation returns its own:\n\n```\ntrait Container {\n    fn items(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = i32>;\n}\n```\nThis used to require `Box<dyn Iterator>` or an associated type. Now it's just `impl`. It's called **RPITIT** (return-position impl Trait in traits)."},{"t":"code","code":"trait Container {\n    fn items(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = i32>;\n    fn total(&self) -> i32 {\n        self.items().sum()\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Evens(i32);\nstruct List(Vec<i32>);\n\nimpl Container for Evens {\n    fn items(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = i32> {\n        (0..=self.0).filter(|x| x % 2 == 0)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Container for List {\n    fn items(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = i32> {\n        self.0.iter().copied()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Evens(6).total());\n    println!(\"{}\", List(vec![5, 5, 1]).total());\n}","out":"12\n11","after":"`Evens` returns a `Filter<...>`, `List` returns a `Copied<...>`. Different types, one trait."},{"t":"text","title":"async fn in a trait","md":"In the same way, you can write `async fn` right in a trait:\n\n```\ntrait Storage {\n    async fn load(&self, key: &str) -> Option<String>;\n}\n```\nUnder the hood it's the same trick: the method returns an `impl Future<Output = ...>`.\n\nOne caveat: such a trait is **not dyn compatible** (`impl` is a concrete but hidden type). For `dyn`, use the `async-trait` crate or a hand-written `Pin<Box<dyn Future>>`."},{"t":"code","md":"Let's check it with the home-made `block_on` from Level 2:","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::pin;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\ntrait Storage {\n    async fn load(&self, key: &str) -> Option<String>;\n}\n\nstruct Memory;\n\nimpl Storage for Memory {\n    async fn load(&self, key: &str) -> Option<String> {\n        match key {\n            \"name\" => Some(String::from(\"Ferris\")),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn greet<S: Storage>(s: &S) -> String {\n    let name = s.load(\"name\").await.unwrap_or_default();\n    format!(\"Hello, {name}!\")\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", block_on(greet(&Memory)));\n}","out":"Hello, Ferris!"},{"t":"quiz","md":"Can types with `async fn` in a trait go into a `Vec<Box<dyn Storage>>`?","options":["Yes, no problem","No, `dyn` needs `async-trait` or a hand-written `Pin<Box<dyn Future>>`","Only for `Box`, not for `&`"],"answer":1,"why":["`async fn` in a trait returns a hidden `impl Future`; that's not dyn compatible.","Right.","The kind of pointer doesn't matter."]},{"t":"task","md":"Add a method `long_words(&self, min: usize) -> impl Iterator<Item = &str>` to the `Words` trait that returns words longer than `min` characters. Implement it for `Sentence`.","code":"trait Words {\n    fn long_words(&self, min: usize) -> impl Iterator<Item = &str>;\n}\n\nstruct Sentence(String);\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let s = Sentence(String::from(\"the cat ate tremendous pies\"));\n    let v: Vec<&str> = s.long_words(4).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"trait Words {\n    fn long_words(&self, min: usize) -> impl Iterator<Item = &str>;\n}\n\nstruct Sentence(String);\n\nimpl Words for Sentence {\n    fn long_words(&self, min: usize) -> impl Iterator<Item = &str> {\n        self.0.split_whitespace().filter(move |w| w.chars().count() > min)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let s = Sentence(String::from(\"the cat ate tremendous pies\"));\n    let v: Vec<&str> = s.long_words(4).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"[\"tremendous\"]","hints":["`self.0.split_whitespace().filter(move |w| w.chars().count() > min)`.","`move` is needed so the closure takes `min` with it."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`fn x(&self) -> impl Trait` right in a trait — each implementation has its own type.","`async fn` in a trait — the same, but with a `Future`.","Such traits aren't dyn compatible."]}],"goals":["Return `impl Trait` from trait methods","Write `async fn` right in a trait"]},{"id":"x02-p","title":"Practice: fine points of traits","min":20,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Plugins: a `Plugin` trait with `name(&self) -> &str` and `run(&self, input: i32) -> i32`, plus a constructor `create() -> Self`. Make the trait dyn compatible, implement two plugins (`Double`: multiplies by 2, `Inc`: adds 1) and run the number 5 through all of them in order.","code":"trait Plugin {\n    fn create() -> Self;\n    fn name(&self) -> &str;\n    fn run(&self, input: i32) -> i32;\n}\n\n// your code: Double and Inc\n\nfn main() {\n    let chain: Vec<Box<dyn Plugin>> = vec![Box::new(Double::create()), Box::new(Inc::create())];\n    let mut x = 5;\n    for p in &chain {\n        x = p.run(x);\n        println!(\"{} -> {x}\", p.name());\n    }\n}","solution":"trait Plugin {\n    fn create() -> Self\n    where\n        Self: Sized;\n    fn name(&self) -> &str;\n    fn run(&self, input: i32) -> i32;\n}\n\nstruct Double;\nstruct Inc;\n\nimpl Plugin for Double {\n    fn create() -> Self { Double }\n    fn name(&self) -> &str { \"double\" }\n    fn run(&self, input: i32) -> i32 { input * 2 }\n}\n\nimpl Plugin for Inc {\n    fn create() -> Self { Inc }\n    fn name(&self) -> &str { \"inc\" }\n    fn run(&self, input: i32) -> i32 { input + 1 }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let chain: Vec<Box<dyn Plugin>> = vec![Box::new(Double::create()), Box::new(Inc::create())];\n    let mut x = 5;\n    for p in &chain {\n        x = p.run(x);\n        println!(\"{} -> {x}\", p.name());\n    }\n}","expect":"double -> 10\ninc -> 11","hints":["`fn create() -> Self where Self: Sized;` — otherwise the trait isn't dyn compatible."]},{"t":"task","md":"An event bus with `Any`: `handle(event: &dyn Any)` prints `click (x, y)` for `Click`, `key K` for `Key`, and `unknown` for anything else.","code":"use std::any::Any;\n\nstruct Click {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\nstruct Key(char);\n\nfn handle(event: &dyn Any) {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let events: Vec<Box<dyn Any>> = vec![Box::new(Click { x: 3, y: 4 }), Box::new(Key('q')), Box::new(42u8)];\n    for e in &events {\n        handle(e.as_ref());\n    }\n}","solution":"use std::any::Any;\n\nstruct Click {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\nstruct Key(char);\n\nfn handle(event: &dyn Any) {\n    if let Some(c) = event.downcast_ref::<Click>() {\n        println!(\"click ({}, {})\", c.x, c.y);\n    } else if let Some(k) = event.downcast_ref::<Key>() {\n        println!(\"key {}\", k.0);\n    } else {\n        println!(\"unknown\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let events: Vec<Box<dyn Any>> = vec![Box::new(Click { x: 3, y: 4 }), Box::new(Key('q')), Box::new(42u8)];\n    for e in &events {\n        handle(e.as_ref());\n    }\n}","expect":"click (3, 4)\nkey q\nunknown","hints":["A chain: `if let Some(c) = event.downcast_ref::<Click>() { ... } else if ...`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A `LendingIterator` over chunks of a slice: `ChunksMut` yields `&mut [i32]` of `n` items. Use it to reverse every chunk.","code":"trait LendingIterator {\n    type Item<'a>\n    where\n        Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item<'_>>;\n}\n\nstruct ChunksMut<'s> {\n    data: &'s mut [i32],\n    n: usize,\n    pos: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<'s> LendingIterator for ChunksMut<'s> {\n    type Item<'a> = &'a mut [i32] where Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&mut [i32]> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut v = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\n    let mut it = ChunksMut { data: &mut v, n: 2, pos: 0 };\n    while let Some(c) = it.next() {\n        c.reverse();\n    }\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"trait LendingIterator {\n    type Item<'a>\n    where\n        Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item<'_>>;\n}\n\nstruct ChunksMut<'s> {\n    data: &'s mut [i32],\n    n: usize,\n    pos: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<'s> LendingIterator for ChunksMut<'s> {\n    type Item<'a> = &'a mut [i32] where Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&mut [i32]> {\n        if self.pos >= self.data.len() {\n            return None;\n        }\n        let start = self.pos;\n        let end = (start + self.n).min(self.data.len());\n        self.pos = end;\n        Some(&mut self.data[start..end])\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut v = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\n    let mut it = ChunksMut { data: &mut v, n: 2, pos: 0 };\n    while let Some(c) = it.next() {\n        c.reverse();\n    }\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"[2, 1, 4, 3, 5]","hints":["The end of a chunk is `(start + n).min(len)`, so the last chunk doesn't go past the end."]},{"t":"task","md":"A `Source` trait with a method `numbers(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32>` and a default method `max_or_zero`. Implement it for `Range10` (the numbers 1..=10) and `Fixed` (its own vector). Print the maximums.","code":"trait Source {\n    fn numbers(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32>;\n    fn max_or_zero(&self) -> u32 {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Range10;\nstruct Fixed(Vec<u32>);\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Range10.max_or_zero());\n    println!(\"{}\", Fixed(vec![]).max_or_zero());\n    println!(\"{}\", Fixed(vec![4, 9, 2]).max_or_zero());\n}","solution":"trait Source {\n    fn numbers(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32>;\n    fn max_or_zero(&self) -> u32 {\n        self.numbers().max().unwrap_or(0)\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Range10;\nstruct Fixed(Vec<u32>);\n\nimpl Source for Range10 {\n    fn numbers(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32> {\n        1..=10\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Source for Fixed {\n    fn numbers(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32> {\n        self.0.iter().copied()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Range10.max_or_zero());\n    println!(\"{}\", Fixed(vec![]).max_or_zero());\n    println!(\"{}\", Fixed(vec![4, 9, 2]).max_or_zero());\n}","expect":"10\n0\n9","hints":["`self.numbers().max().unwrap_or(0)`.","For `Fixed` — `self.0.iter().copied()`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`where Self: Sized` — constructors in dyn-compatible traits.","`downcast_ref` — handling mixed events.","GATs let you change data in chunks through your own iterator.","`impl Iterator` in a trait — flexible data sources."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Use dyn compatibility, upcasting, Any, GATs and RPITIT"]}]},{"id":"x03","title":"Subtyping, variance and fighting the borrow checker","sub":"Why &'static fits where &'a is expected but &mut doesn't, and everyday tricks","lessons":[{"id":"x03-1","title":"Lifetime subtyping","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Long fits where short is expected","md":"Rust has almost no subtyping, except in one place — **lifetimes**. If a reference lives longer, you can pass it where a shorter one is expected.\n\n```\nfn show<'a>(s: &'a str) { println!(\"{s}\"); }\nlet lit: &'static str = \"an eternal string\";\nshow(lit); // &'static str fits where &'a str is expected\n```\nWe say `'static` is a **subtype** of any `'a`. It's written `'static: 'a` — \"`'static` lives at least as long as `'a`\".\n\n> **Analogy:** a yearly pass works where a daily pass is required. Not the other way round."},{"t":"text","title":"How to read 'a: 'b and T: 'a","md":"- `'a: 'b` — \"`'a` lives **at least as long as** `'b`\".\n- `T: 'a` — \"`T` holds no references that die before `'a`\".\n\n```\nstruct Holder<'a, T: 'a> {\n    item: &'a T,\n}\n```\nThese days the compiler often infers such bounds by itself, but they show up in error messages all the time."},{"t":"code","code":"fn pick_first<'long: 'short, 'short>(a: &'long str, _b: &'short str) -> &'short str {\n    a // 'long lives longer, so it fits as 'short\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let outer = String::from(\"outside\");\n    let r;\n    {\n        let inner = String::from(\"inside\");\n        r = pick_first(&outer, &inner);\n        println!(\"{r}\");\n    }\n}","out":"outside","after":"The result is labeled with the short term `'short`, so it can't be used after the block — even though it really points to `outer`. The labels honestly say \"no longer than the short one\"."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `'a: 'b` mean?","options":["`'a` lives at least as long as `'b`","`'a` lives shorter than `'b`","`'a` and `'b` are the same"],"answer":0,"why":["Right.","The opposite.","Not necessarily: `'a` may be longer."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `choose` returns one of two `&str`, and `main` stores its result in a variable that lives longer than the block. Add labels so the code compiles.","code":"fn choose<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str, first: bool) -> &'a str {\n    if first { a } else { b }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let saved: &'static str;\n    {\n        let tmp = String::from(\"temporary\");\n        let _ = choose(&tmp, \"x\", true);\n        saved = choose(\"first\", \"second\", false);\n    }\n    println!(\"{saved}\");\n}","solution":"fn choose<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str, first: bool) -> &'a str {\n    if first { a } else { b }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let saved: &'static str;\n    {\n        let tmp = String::from(\"temporary\");\n        let _ = choose(&tmp, \"x\", true);\n        saved = choose(\"first\", \"second\", false);\n    }\n    println!(\"{saved}\");\n}","expect":"second","hints":["A trap: the code is already correct! In the second call both arguments are `'static`, so `'a = 'static` and the result is `'static` too. Just run it."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A long reference fits where a short one is expected: `'static` fits everywhere.","`'a: 'b` — `'a` lives at least as long as `'b`. `T: 'a` — `T` holds no shorter references.","A result's label is a promise of \"no longer than\", not its real term."]}],"goals":["See that a longer-lived reference fits where a shorter one is expected","Read the bounds `'a: 'b` and `T: 'a`"]},{"id":"x03-2","title":"Variance","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Three kinds","md":"**Variance** answers the question: if `'long` fits where `'short` is expected, does `Type<'long>` fit where `Type<'short>` is expected?\n\n- **Covariance** — yes. `&'a T`, `Box<T>`, `Vec<T>`, `Option<T>`. Almost everything.\n- **Invariance** — no, only an exact match. `&mut T`, `Cell<T>`, `RefCell<T>`, `UnsafeCell<T>`.\n- **Contravariance** — the reverse. It only shows up in function arguments: `fn(T)`.\n\n> **Analogy:** a box with a yearly pass can go to someone expecting a box with a daily pass — they'll only look. But if the box is given so they can **put** something of theirs into it, swapping is dangerous: they'll put a daily pass where you expect a yearly one."},{"t":"text","title":"Why &mut is invariant","md":"If `&mut &'static str` fitted where `&mut &'short str` is expected, you could write a **short** reference where everyone thinks an **eternal** one lives:\n\n```\nfn put<'a>(slot: &mut &'a str, s: &'a str) { *slot = s; }\n\nlet mut forever: &'static str = \"eternal\";\n{\n    let tmp = String::from(\"temporary\");\n    put(&mut forever, &tmp); // error! otherwise forever would point to a dead tmp\n}\n```\nThe invariance of `&mut` closes that hole."},{"t":"code","fails":true,"md":"Run it and look at the error — that's invariance protecting you:","code":"fn put<'a>(slot: &mut &'a str, s: &'a str) {\n    *slot = s;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut forever: &'static str = \"eternal\";\n    {\n        let tmp = String::from(\"temporary\");\n        put(&mut forever, &tmp);\n    }\n    println!(\"{forever}\");\n}","after":"The compiler says: `tmp` does not live long enough. Through `&mut` you can't \"shorten\" a term, so you can't smuggle a short reference into a long-lived place."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which type is invariant in `T`?","options":["`&'a T`","`Vec<T>`","`Cell<T>`"],"answer":2,"why":["Covariant: read-only.","Covariant.","Right. You can write through a `Cell`, so swapping is dangerous."]},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why can a `Vec<&'static str>` go where a `Vec<&'a str>` is expected, but a `&mut Vec<&'static str>` can't go where a `&mut Vec<&'a str>` is expected?","options":["Through `&mut`, the function could put a short reference into the vector","A `Vec` can't be changed","It's a compiler bug"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. An owned `Vec` is handed over whole — no hole.","It can, but ownership goes to whoever received it.","It's a deliberate safety rule."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `add_word` puts a reference to a word into a list. The list and the words in `main` live equally long. Add labels so the code compiles.","code":"fn add_word(list: &mut Vec<&str>, w: &str) {\n    list.push(w);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = String::from(\"fish whale crab\");\n    let mut words = Vec::new();\n    for w in text.split(' ') {\n        add_word(&mut words, w);\n    }\n    println!(\"{words:?}\");\n}","solution":"fn add_word<'a>(list: &mut Vec<&'a str>, w: &'a str) {\n    list.push(w);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = String::from(\"fish whale crab\");\n    let mut words = Vec::new();\n    for w in text.split(' ') {\n        add_word(&mut words, w);\n    }\n    println!(\"{words:?}\");\n}","expect":"[\"fish\", \"whale\", \"crab\"]","hints":["The word goes **inside** the list, so it has the same term as the elements: `list: &mut Vec<&'a str>, w: &'a str`.","The reference to the list itself (`&mut`) doesn't need its own label."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Covariant: `&T`, `Box<T>`, `Vec<T>` — long fits where short is expected.","Invariant: `&mut T`, `Cell<T>` — only an exact match.","Invariance protects against writing a short reference into a long-lived place."]}],"goals":["See where subtyping \"passes through\" a type and where it doesn't","Learn why `&mut` is stricter than `&`"]},{"id":"x03-3","title":"PhantomData and variance","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"PhantomData decides three things","md":"When a struct has a raw pointer or a parameter that isn't stored, `PhantomData` tells the compiler **how** to treat it:\n\n- `PhantomData<T>` — \"I sort of own a `T`\": covariant, `Send/Sync` like `T`, `T` may be dropped along with it.\n- `PhantomData<&'a T>` — \"I borrow a `T` for `'a`\".\n- `PhantomData<fn() -> T>` — \"I only produce `T`s\": covariant, but doesn't affect `Send/Sync` or dropping.\n- `PhantomData<*const T>` — \"like a raw pointer\": removes `Send` and `Sync`."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::marker::PhantomData;\n\nstruct OwnsT<T>(PhantomData<T>);\nstruct NotThreadSafe(PhantomData<*const u8>);\n\nfn is_send<T: Send>() -> bool { true }\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", is_send::<OwnsT<String>>());\n    // println!(\"{}\", is_send::<NotThreadSafe>()); // error: *const u8 isn't Send\n    let _x = NotThreadSafe(PhantomData);\n    println!(\"sizes: {} {}\", std::mem::size_of::<OwnsT<String>>(), std::mem::size_of::<NotThreadSafe>());\n}","out":"true\nsizes: 0 0","after":"Uncomment the line and see: `PhantomData<*const u8>` made the type unusable across threads. Sometimes that's exactly what you want — say, for a handle that must stay on one thread."},{"t":"quiz","md":"You need to forbid sending a type to another thread without adding data. What goes in the field?","options":["`PhantomData<u8>`","`PhantomData<*const ()>`","`PhantomData<String>`"],"answer":1,"why":["`u8` is `Send`, nothing changes.","Right. Raw pointers are neither `Send` nor `Sync`.","`String` is `Send`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Drop check","md":"When a type with `Drop` is dropped, the compiler checks that `drop` won't touch data that's already dead. This is the **drop check**.\n\nFor your own collections built on raw pointers, it's important to tell the compiler \"I own `T`\" with `PhantomData<T>`. Then it understands that dropping the collection drops the elements too, and checks the lifetimes correctly.\n\nThe standard library sometimes relaxes the check with a special attribute `#[may_dangle]` (nightly only). Ordinary code doesn't need it — the right `PhantomData` is enough."},{"t":"recap","items":["`PhantomData<T>` — I own; `<&'a T>` — I borrow; `<fn() -> T>` — I only produce.","`PhantomData<*const T>` removes `Send` and `Sync`.","`PhantomData` always has size zero."]}],"goals":["Choose the right kind of `PhantomData`","Know how it affects `Send`, `Sync` and dropping"]},{"id":"x03-4","title":"Tricks against fighting the borrow checker","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Trick 1: borrow fields separately","md":"The compiler tells a struct's **fields** apart. `&mut self.a` and `&self.b` at the same time — fine. But taking the whole `&mut self` through a method — not fine.\n\n```\n// bad: the method borrows all of self\nlet first = self.first_item();\nself.counter += 1; // error if first is still alive\n// good: separate fields\nlet first = &self.items[0];\nself.counter += 1; // ok\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"Trick 2: an index instead of a reference","md":"Don't keep a reference to an element — keep its **index**. An index is just a number; it borrows nothing."},{"t":"text","title":"Trick 3: entry instead of get + insert","md":"```\n// bad\nif !map.contains_key(&k) { map.insert(k, 0); }\n*map.get_mut(&k).unwrap() += 1;\n// good\n*map.entry(k).or_insert(0) += 1;\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"Trick 4: read first, then change","md":"Pull the value you need into a variable (copy a number, `clone` a small string), end the borrow — and only then change things.\n\n> **Analogy:** don't try to read a book and re-glue its pages at the same time. Copy the line you need onto a note, close the book — then glue."},{"t":"task","md":"The `promote_first` method must bump the promotion counter and set the `boss` flag for the first employee. Right now it doesn't compile. Fix it by borrowing fields separately.","code":"struct Team {\n    names: Vec<String>,\n    bosses: Vec<bool>,\n    promotions: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Team {\n    fn first_name(&self) -> &String {\n        &self.names[0]\n    }\n    fn promote_first(&mut self) -> String {\n        let name = self.first_name();\n        self.bosses[0] = true;\n        self.promotions += 1;\n        format!(\"{name} is the boss now\")\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut t = Team { names: vec![String::from(\"Ada\")], bosses: vec![false], promotions: 0 };\n    println!(\"{}\", t.promote_first());\n    println!(\"{} {}\", t.bosses[0], t.promotions);\n}","solution":"struct Team {\n    names: Vec<String>,\n    bosses: Vec<bool>,\n    promotions: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Team {\n    fn promote_first(&mut self) -> String {\n        let name = &self.names[0];\n        self.bosses[0] = true;\n        self.promotions += 1;\n        format!(\"{name} is the boss now\")\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut t = Team { names: vec![String::from(\"Ada\")], bosses: vec![false], promotions: 0 };\n    println!(\"{}\", t.promote_first());\n    println!(\"{} {}\", t.bosses[0], t.promotions);\n}","expect":"Ada is the boss now\ntrue 1","hints":["`self.first_name()` borrows all of `self`. Take the field directly: `let name = &self.names[0];`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Find the index of the longest word and **append** `!` to it right in the vector. You can't hold a reference — use the index.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut words = vec![String::from(\"cat\"), String::from(\"hippo\"), String::from(\"ox\")];\n    // your code\n    println!(\"{words:?}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut words = vec![String::from(\"cat\"), String::from(\"hippo\"), String::from(\"ox\")];\n    let idx = (0..words.len()).max_by_key(|&i| words[i].chars().count()).unwrap();\n    words[idx].push('!');\n    println!(\"{words:?}\");\n}","expect":"[\"cat\", \"hippo!\", \"ox\"]","hints":["`(0..words.len()).max_by_key(|&i| words[i].chars().count())` returns the index.","Then `words[idx].push('!')` — the borrow used for searching is already over."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Separate fields — fine; all of `self` through a method — not fine.","An index instead of a reference.","`entry` instead of `get` + `insert`.","Read first and end the borrow, then change."]}],"goals":["Know four common tricks that make borrow errors go away","Solve tasks without extra `clone()`s"]},{"id":"x03-p","title":"Practice: borrowing","min":20,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Count letter frequencies in a text into a `BTreeMap<char, u32>` using `entry`. Skip spaces.","code":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"banana bar\";\n    let mut freq: BTreeMap<char, u32> = BTreeMap::new();\n    // your code\n    println!(\"{freq:?}\");\n}","solution":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"banana bar\";\n    let mut freq: BTreeMap<char, u32> = BTreeMap::new();\n    for c in text.chars().filter(|c| !c.is_whitespace()) {\n        *freq.entry(c).or_insert(0) += 1;\n    }\n    println!(\"{freq:?}\");\n}","expect":"{'a': 4, 'b': 2, 'n': 2, 'r': 1}","hints":["`*freq.entry(c).or_insert(0) += 1;`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The `transfer` method moves money between two accounts in one vector. You can't directly get two `&mut` to elements of the same vector — use `split_at_mut` or work by indexes.","code":"struct Bank {\n    accounts: Vec<i64>,\n}\n\nimpl Bank {\n    fn transfer(&mut self, from: usize, to: usize, amount: i64) {\n        let a = &mut self.accounts[from];\n        let b = &mut self.accounts[to];\n        *a -= amount;\n        *b += amount;\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut bank = Bank { accounts: vec![100, 50, 0] };\n    bank.transfer(0, 2, 30);\n    bank.transfer(1, 0, 10);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", bank.accounts);\n}","solution":"struct Bank {\n    accounts: Vec<i64>,\n}\n\nimpl Bank {\n    fn transfer(&mut self, from: usize, to: usize, amount: i64) {\n        self.accounts[from] -= amount;\n        self.accounts[to] += amount;\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut bank = Bank { accounts: vec![100, 50, 0] };\n    bank.transfer(0, 2, 30);\n    bank.transfer(1, 0, 10);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", bank.accounts);\n}","expect":"[80, 40, 30]","hints":["Simplest: don't hold two references: `self.accounts[from] -= amount; self.accounts[to] += amount;`.","If you really need two `&mut` at once — `get_disjoint_mut([from, to])` or `split_at_mut`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A cache: `get_or_compute` returns a `&str` from the map, and if the key is missing it computes the value (the key in uppercase) and stores it. Fix the version that doesn't compile.","code":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nstruct Cache {\n    map: HashMap<String, String>,\n    misses: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Cache {\n    fn get_or_compute(&mut self, key: &str) -> &str {\n        if let Some(v) = self.map.get(key) {\n            return v;\n        }\n        self.misses += 1;\n        self.map.insert(key.to_string(), key.to_uppercase());\n        self.map.get(key).unwrap()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = Cache { map: HashMap::new(), misses: 0 };\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get_or_compute(\"rust\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get_or_compute(\"rust\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get_or_compute(\"crab\"));\n    println!(\"misses: {}\", c.misses);\n}","solution":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nstruct Cache {\n    map: HashMap<String, String>,\n    misses: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Cache {\n    fn get_or_compute(&mut self, key: &str) -> &str {\n        if !self.map.contains_key(key) {\n            self.misses += 1;\n        }\n        self.map.entry(key.to_string()).or_insert_with(|| key.to_uppercase())\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = Cache { map: HashMap::new(), misses: 0 };\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get_or_compute(\"rust\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get_or_compute(\"rust\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get_or_compute(\"crab\"));\n    println!(\"misses: {}\", c.misses);\n}","expect":"RUST\nRUST\nCRAB\nmisses: 2","hints":["This is a known limitation of the checker: the early `return v` from `get` keeps the borrow alive until the end of the function.","Split it into \"check\" (`contains_key`) and \"insert or take\" (`entry(...).or_insert_with(...)`)."]},{"t":"task","mustContain":["-> &str"],"md":"The `longest_line` function returns the longest line of a text. Right now it takes a `&String` and returns a `String` via `clone`. Rewrite it with no copies: take `&str`, return `&str`.","code":"fn longest_line(text: &String) -> String {\n    let mut best = String::new();\n    for line in text.lines() {\n        if line.len() > best.len() {\n            best = line.to_string();\n        }\n    }\n    best.clone()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let t = String::from(\"short\\nthe longest line here\\nmedium\");\n    println!(\"{}\", longest_line(&t));\n}","solution":"fn longest_line(text: &str) -> &str {\n    let mut best = \"\";\n    for line in text.lines() {\n        if line.len() > best.len() {\n            best = line;\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let t = String::from(\"short\\nthe longest line here\\nmedium\");\n    println!(\"{}\", longest_line(&t));\n}","expect":"the longest line here","hints":["One reference in — no labels needed: `fn longest_line(text: &str) -> &str`.","`let mut best = \"\";` and assign `best = line;` — they're all pieces of the original text."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`entry` — counts and caches without a double lookup.","Indexes — when you need to change two elements of one collection.","An early `return` of a reference is a common trap; `entry` saves the day.","`&str` instead of `String` — fewer copies."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Fix several typical borrow errors without extra copies"]}]}],"glossary":[]};
